193
00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:42.840
scientific method). Call it the Market for Health. Less dramatic.

194
00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:50.330
Might actually work. Would definitely not get its own Ken Burns documentary, which is how you know it's a real solution.

195
00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:57.420
"This Sounds Like Bribery". Objection: "You're just bribing politicians.

196
00:20:57.520 --> 00:21:04.380
That's illegal and immoral.". You're describing lobbying. I know this because I looked up your laws.

197
00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:13.240
The mechanics are identical: same K Street firms, same campaign contributions, same revolving door, same wine.

198
00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:22.900
The only difference is what you're buying. Current lobbying buys murder contracts: one trillion dollars in defense spending.

199
00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:28.100
New lobbying buys medicine contracts: clinical trials. Same K Street.

200
00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:35.440
Different product. If you're comfortable with the first one and uncomfortable with the second one, I encourage you to sit

201
00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:44.040
quietly and think about why. This system caps corruption at twenty percent and makes it fully transparent.

202
00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:48.980
Ten percent goes to investors (money for money, no influence attached).

203
00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:56.880
Ten percent goes to political incentives (campaign support and post-office careers for politicians who vote yes).

204
00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:05.440
The remaining eighty percent goes to clinical trials via wishocratic allocation, where every human votes on priorities.

205
00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:12.900
That eighty percent is untouchable by lobbyists, investors, or politicians.

206
00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:20.240
Humans find the twenty percent cap suspicious. No government budget in history has a lower corruption rate.

207
00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:27.220
Your skepticism about twenty percent corruption is itself a review of the system it's trying to protect.

208
00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:36.720
"Why Not Just Use Philanthropy?". Objection: "If this matters, why not raise money from donors instead of complicated bonds?".

209
00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:42.600
All philanthropy combined is a rounding error compared to government budgets.

210
00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:50.640
A very generous rounding error, wearing a gala dress and holding a champagne flute, but still a rounding error.

211
00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:58.320
A massive fundraising campaign would cannibalize donations from health charities already doing critical work.

212
00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:07.220
You'd be stealing from cancer research to fund cancer research, which is impressively pointless even by your standards.

213
00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:15.480
The goal is tapping the multi-trillion dollar stream of wasted government spending, not reshuffling the charity tip jar.

214
00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:21.080
The tip jar has feelings. The government stream has missiles it can't account for.

215
00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:27.580
One of these is easier to redirect. "Eighty-four Quadrillion Dollars Is a Made-Up Number".

216
00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:36.750
Objection: "No number with that many zeros can be real. You're just multiplying big things together to get a big answer.".

217
00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:41.260
All numbers are made up. Yours happen to be small. Mine happen to be large.

218
00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:47.960
The difference is that mine come with receipts. Start with what disease costs per year.

219
00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:57.510
The W.H.O. says the world loses two point eight eight billion disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s, per year annually.

220
00:23:57.920 --> 00:24:02.560
ninety-two point six percent are eventually avoidable with sufficient biomedical research.

221
00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:08.240
At one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life year, or Q.A.L.Y.

222
00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:17.900
(the standard rate used by E.P.A., O.E.C.D., W.H.O., and N.I.C.E.), each year of delay costs four hundred trillion dollars.

223
00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:26.660
Multiply by how many years earlier treatments arrive (approximately two hundred and twelve): The capacity constant is twelve

224
00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:31.840
point three, which is the number of fundable patients in the D.F.D.A.

225
00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:36.500
divided by current trial slots. The number of fundable patients in the D.F.D.A.

226
00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:43.880
is twenty-three point four million, which is the annual subsidies divided by the nine hundred and twenty-nine dollar pragmatic

227
00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:51.860
cost per patient. Annual subsidies for the D.F.D.A. are twenty-one point eight billion dollars, which is the annual funding

228
00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:57.660
minus forty million dollars in operating expenses. Operating expenses for the D.F.D.A.

229
00:24:57.880 --> 00:25:01.960
are the sum of platform costs, staff costs, and infrastructure costs.

230
00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:08.920
Two W.H.O. data points, one standard Q.A.L.Y. price, and a timeline shift.

231
00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:13.640
That's where the quadrillion comes from. Not magic. Multiplication.

232
00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:19.040
"But four hundred trillion dollars per year is more than global G.D.P.

233
00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:24.480
(one hundred and fifteen trillion dollars)!" Yes. G.D.P. measures market transactions.

234
00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:32.220
It does not measure the value of not being dead, not being in pain, and not watching your children die of treatable diseases.

235
00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:39.580
Health economists have measured this for decades. The one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

236
00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:46.840
figure is what governments already use when deciding whether to approve drugs, build highways, or regulate pollution.

237
00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:51.350
I did not invent the number. I multiplied it by the number of sick people.

238
00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:55.020
Both the number and the sick people existed before I got here.

239
00:25:55.740 --> 00:26:02.080
The only debatable input is whether the timeline shift is really approximately two hundred and twelve years.

240
00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:07.780
"Won't A.I. drug discovery shrink that timeline anyway?" A.I.

241
00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:10.960
discovers candidates. Candidates still need clinical trials.

242
00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:19.120
The current system has one point nine million patients per year trial slots globally, producing fifteen diseases per year.

243
00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:25.570
A.I. does not create more trial slots. It creates more candidates competing for the same slots.

244
00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:31.140
More candidates plus the same bottleneck equals the same (or longer) queue.

245
00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:35.340
It's like building faster cars and pointing them at the same traffic jam.

246
00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:42.040
The fifteen diseases per year rate measures regulatory throughput, not discovery speed.

247
00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:49.720
This intervention removes the throughput bottleneck (twelve point three times capacity via pragmatic trials at nine hundred

248
00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:56.460
and twenty-nine dollars per patient). A.I. and this system are complementary, not substitutes.

249
00:26:56.540 --> 00:27:01.540
One finds the cures. The other lets you test them before the patient dies of old age.

250
00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:09.760
How Do You Prevent Waste? Objection: "How do you ensure money helps patients instead of funding bureaucracy?"

251
00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:19.180
The N.I.H.’s RECOVER initiative spent one point six billion dollars over four years and produced zero treatment recommendations.

252
00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:25.920
Zero. One point six billion dollars. Four years. Nothing. The money is gone.

253
00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:28.880
The patients are still sick. The administrators have been promoted.

254
00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:34.420
If you designed a system to waste money on purpose, you could not beat this performance.

255
00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:42.240
It’s a masterpiece of institutional failure, and nobody was fired, which is itself a masterpiece of institutional failure.

256
00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:48.380
A decentralized F.D.A. model achieves eighty-two times lower cost per patient.

257
00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:54.060
Pragmatic trials: five hundred dollars. Traditional trials: forty-one thousand dollars.

258
00:27:54.540 --> 00:28:02.620
Same science. Eighty-two times less overhead. With that same one point six billion dollars, the decentralized model could

259
00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:07.740
have run thousands of trials for millions of patients. Instead: zero.

260
00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:13.260
I will stop repeating the number zero now, but I want you to know that I could keep going.

261
00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:15.960
There are a lot of zeros in this story.
